<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
    Kod's hardened-runtime entitlements for Developer ID distribution
    (SPEC 17 M4: "Signed/notarized distribution"). Kod is not sandboxed
    (it is a Developer-ID-distributed app, not a Mac App Store one), so
    no com.apple.security.app-sandbox entitlement is present, and this
    file is deliberately as close to empty as a real release needs:

    - Kod never loads a JIT-compiled or self-modifying code region, so
      no com.apple.security.cs.allow-jit / allow-unsigned-executable-
      memory entitlement is requested.
    - Kod never loads a third-party plug-in/dylib into its own process
      (SPEC 1.2: "no extension runtime or arbitrary in-process plug-in
      API"), so no com.apple.security.cs.disable-library-validation
      entitlement is requested.
    - Every subprocess Kod launches (git, a language server, ripgrep) is
      a separate process, not code loaded into Kod's own address space,
      so hardened runtime's library-validation/code-signing protections
      for Kod's *own* process are unaffected by what those subprocesses
      are or aren't signed with, and no entitlement is needed for that
      either.
    - Kod's network use is limited to explicit app-update and opt-in
      crash-report submission and remote Markdown image loading (SPEC
      13.3); com.apple.security.network.client is present so those
      (non-sandboxed, but still worth being explicit and minimal about)
      network paths keep working under any future sandboxing decision
      without silently degrading.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.cs.disable-executable-page-protection</key>
    <false/>
    <key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
